<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chudamani Vihara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1006</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Chulamanivarma Vihara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chudamani Vihara was a Buddhist monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagapattinam">Nagapattinam</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel</a> coast of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built in the early eleventh century by the Srivijayan king Sri Mara Vijayottungavarman. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-i">Rajaraja I</a> granted it the revenue of a village, as recorded in the Larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates. It shows the close religious and commercial ties between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> and the Cholas before their war of 1025; its last ruined tower was demolished in 1867.</p></div>
